{
  "name": "Preceptor's Primer",

  // Distribution model (settled): students launch a Codespace directly ON
  // PPBDS/codespace-starter and run connect-repo.sh to create or clone their
  // own work repo — they do NOT "Use this template", fork, or use Classroom.
  // Because they launch from THIS repo, they get its Codespaces prebuild
  // (enabled in Settings → Codespaces), so first launches are fast. Their work
  // repo is just data, opened as a folder inside the same Codespace; it needs
  // no prebuild of its own.

  // (Operational note: trivial edits near this line re-fire the Codespaces
  // prebuild when a repin merge fails to trigger one. Happened 2026-08-08
  // (GitHub dropped the trigger), 2026-08-09 (merge landed while another
  // prebuild ran; concurrent runs are skipped, not queued), and 2026-08-11
  // (dropped again, no concurrent run — this is a recurring GitHub flake,
  // so KEEP this note). Also note: an ordinary push to main touching this
  // file does not reliably fire a prebuild either — the 2026-08-11 "better
  // start up" push never triggered one.)
  // The PPBDS image, pinned to v1.1.5. Since v1.0.0 there is ONE image for
  // the whole org (ghcr.io/ppbds/devcontainer — it replaced the old
  // devcontainers/base + /dev + /student trio, and additionally bakes the
  // package-development tooling: devtools, pkgdown, roxygen2, testthat,
  // usethis, qpdf). Bumping is a deliberate edit to this line — never use
  // `:latest` in student-facing config, since it would float silently with
  // every push to PPBDS/devcontainers.
  //
  // WHEN BUMPING THIS PIN, bump it everywhere: the same tag is pinned in the
  // CI of the tutorial package repos, where a "student-env-render" job
  // renders every tutorial inside this exact image to prove it works for
  // students. Currently: primer.tutorials, misc.tutorials, and
  // vscode.tutorials (each in .github/workflows/R-CMD-check.yaml).
  // Also PPBDS/primer's .devcontainer pins this image. If those pins lag,
  // their CI validates against an image students no longer run.
  //
  // Bakes in: gh, quarto, arf (Rust R console), httpgd, pak,
  // tutorial.helpers, vscode.tutorials, misc.tutorials, primer.tutorials,
  // a modeling stack (tidymodels + xgboost/lightgbm/catboost/randomForest/
  // ranger/glmnet engines + brms for Bayesian/Stan),
  // a mapping/census stack (sf + tidycensus/tigris for census-tract maps,
  // plus the Kyle Walker toolkit: mapgl for token-free WebGL vector maps via
  // maplibre() + CARTO styles, crsuggest for CRS picks, idbr for
  // international Census data — live tidycensus/idbr queries need each
  // student's free Census API key),
  // an inference-reporting/presentation set used throughout the primer book
  // and tutorials (gt tables, marginaleffects, patchwork, easystats),
  // a basic Python data-science stack
  // (numpy/pandas/matplotlib/seaborn/scikit-learn/statsmodels/jupyter in a venv
  // at /opt/venv, the default `python`), interactive-viz + Shinylive
  // packages (R plotly/leaflet/DT/crosstalk + Python plotly/altair/folium/
  // itables; shiny+shinylive compile to WebAssembly for static hosting),
  // Observable (Quarto renders {ojs} cells out of the box; the `observable`
  // Framework CLI is also installed), and five AI coding CLIs:
  //   - claude  (Anthropic)         — sign in with a Claude plan, or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
  //   - codex   (OpenAI)            — sign in with a ChatGPT plan, or `codex login`
  //   - agy     (Antigravity/Google)— sign in with a Google account, or ANTIGRAVITY_API_KEY
  //   - grok    (Grok Build/xAI)    — sign in with a SuperGrok or X Premium+ plan
  //   - aider   (multi-provider)    — key-only: DeepSeek/OpenRouter for cheap models
  // Signing in on first run is the recommended path; API keys (set as
  // Codespaces user secrets at https://github.com/settings/codespaces) are
  // the fallback. See https://github.com/PPBDS/devcontainers for details.
  //
  // Note for instructors: `gh` is preinstalled and authenticated against
  // the Codespace's home repo via the built-in GITHUB_TOKEN. Pushing to
  // OTHER repos (e.g., cloning a sibling package and pushing to it)
  // requires a one-time `gh auth login` per Codespace — that's by design,
  // not a bug. The built-in token is read-mostly outside the home repo.
  "image": "ghcr.io/ppbds/devcontainer:1.1.5",

  // NO postCreateCommand right now — RETIRED 2026-07-30. All course
  // packages, including primer.tutorials, ship at whatever versions the
  // image baked; the live at-create refresh of primer.tutorials was removed
  // because the baked version is recent enough, making creates faster and
  // GitHub-independent.
  //
  // The discussion below is KEPT ON PURPOSE: it is the recipe if course
  // content ever again needs to move faster than image releases. To revive,
  // restore this line here:
  //
  //   "postCreateCommand": "R -q -e 'pak::pkg_install(\"PPBDS/primer.tutorials\", ask = FALSE)'",
  //
  // and re-read the following before doing so:
  //
  // - It MUST be postCreateCommand, not onCreate/updateContentCommand:
  //   those run at prebuild time and would re-freeze "latest" at the
  //   prebuild, recreating the manual-update problem this solves. postCreate
  //   runs live when each Codespace is created, even from a prebuild. A
  //   failed postCreateCommand doesn't block the Codespace from opening, and
  //   the image's baked copy remains as the fallback.
  //
  // - No upgrade = TRUE: deps are already baked at known-good versions, so
  //   the refresh touches only the named package and avoids re-churning the
  //   learnr/knitr/xfun chain (see the xfun ABI note in the devcontainers
  //   Dockerfile).
  //
  // - ACCEPTED RISK — blast radius: tracking GitHub HEAD means a bad commit
  //   to the refreshed package breaks EVERY new Codespace at once, with no
  //   pinned image to shield us. The mitigation is operational, not config:
  //   revert the offending commit upstream ASAP — that fixes all subsequent
  //   creates immediately, with no image rebuild or pin bump needed.

  // "Setup complete" signal + onboarding, via postAttachCommand — NOT
  // postStartCommand. This distinction is the whole point: in Codespaces,
  // postStartCommand (and postCreateCommand) output is routed to the hidden
  // creation log, so a banner there is invisible — students finish the wait
  // staring at a bare prompt. postAttachCommand runs in a VISIBLE terminal,
  // and the lifecycle order (postCreate → postStart → postAttach) means it
  // fires after any create-time work — so if the postCreateCommand above is
  // ever revived, the banner stays a true "ready" signal. The banner shows
  // ONE next action (`connect-repo <name>` — welcome.sh installs that
  // wrapper on PATH); STUDENT_WORKFLOW.md is taught in class, deliberately
  // neither linked in the banner nor auto-opened.
  // Absolute path via ${containerWorkspaceFolder} so it still resolves after a
  // student switches their open folder to their own repo. See welcome.sh.
  //
  // Object form (not a bare string) so the terminal tab is labeled with the
  // short key instead of the full command line — Codespaces names the
  // postAttach terminal after this string. Still a single command; the object
  // form here is purely for the label. The platform prepends "Codespaces: ",
  // so the tab reads "Codespaces: Welcome!" — the prefix is not removable.
  "postAttachCommand": {
    "Welcome!": "bash ${containerWorkspaceFolder}/.devcontainer/welcome.sh"
  },

  "customizations": {
    "vscode": {

      // Extensions students actually need.
      //
      // DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the AI assistant *extensions* (anthropic.claude-code,
      // openai.chatgpt). The AI tools ship as CLIs in the image (claude, codex,
      // agy, aider) and that is intentional — do NOT add the extension panels
      // here. Tested 2026-06: the in-editor panels are webviews that render
      // broken in the browser-based Codespaces client (garbled/overlapping UI),
      // and there is no reliable desktop fallback for a big slice of students —
      // VS Code Desktop does not exist for iPad or ChromeOS, and drops Windows
      // 7/8. The integrated terminal (and thus the CLIs) renders fine on every
      // device, so CLIs are the device-agnostic answer. (Same Codespaces-remote
      // webview class of failure that got the Gemini companion extension dropped.)
      // NOTE — PPBDS.vscode-r-tutorials is deliberately NOT in this list.
      // We publish it to Open VSX, and this list can only install from the
      // Microsoft Marketplace (whose copy is stale). The extension is baked
      // into the image instead (RT_EXT_VERSION build arg in the
      // PPBDS/devcontainers Dockerfile), pre-extracted from the Open VSX
      // .vsix so its Activity Bar icon exists from first window paint — an
      // attach-time install needed a window reload. Ship a new version by
      // bumping RT_EXT_VERSION there and cutting an image release.
      "extensions": [
        // PINNED to 2.8.8 on purpose (2026-08-14). vscode-R 3.0.0 (in rc as
        // of May 2026) replaces the session-watcher architecture with a
        // bundled "sess" R package the image does not bake — an unpinned
        // extension would auto-update every student Codespace the day it
        // hits the Marketplace, breaking plotting with an install prompt
        // students won't understand. Migrate DELIBERATELY: bake sess in the
        // image, bump this pin, and drop the image's Rprofile.site .First
        // shim (which 3.0.0 makes obsolete) in the same release. See
        // REditorSupport/vscode-R#1696 for the R-4.6 background.
        "reditorsupport.r@2.8.8",
        "quarto.quarto",
        "ritwickdey.LiveServer",
        "tomoki1207.pdf",
        "mechatroner.rainbow-csv"
        // ALSO DELIBERATELY ABSENT (dropped 2026-08-20, David): the Python
        // extensions (ms-python.python, ms-toolsai.jupyter). Students use
        // only a handful of Activity Bar icons, and these two added Python/
        // Jupyter icons plus Welcome-page walkthrough cards for tooling the
        // course doesn't teach interactively. Removing them does NOT break
        // the Python workflow that matters: `quarto render` of .qmd files
        // with Python chunks drives the image's Jupyter engine from the CLI,
        // no editor extension involved. What's lost is only the in-editor
        // layer: .ipynb notebook UI, run-buttons on Python cells, Python
        // IntelliSense. If the syllabus later adds interactive Python,
        // re-add both lines AND re-add the python.defaultInterpreterPath /
        // python.terminal.activateEnvironment settings (see git history at
        // this line for the exact blocks and their reasoning — especially
        // activateEnvironment: false, which prevented the extension from
        // corrupting the R Tutorial terminal).
      ],

      "settings": {

        // Make Copilot Chat read AGENTS.md from the workspace root. Inert in
        // this launcher (which deliberately has no AGENTS.md); it matters in
        // the student's own repo, where connect-repo.sh places one by default
        // (skippable via --no-agents). Set explicitly because some VS Code
        // versions ship it off. Canonical file: .devcontainer/AGENTS.md.
        "chat.useAgentsMdFile": true,

        // (python.defaultInterpreterPath and python.terminal.activateEnvironment
        // were removed 2026-08-20 along with the Python extensions — see the
        // DELIBERATELY ABSENT note in the extensions list above. Restore both
        // settings if those extensions ever return.)

        // Blank the built-in, repo-scoped GITHUB_TOKEN / GH_TOKEN in integrated
        // terminals — INCLUDING the R console (arf), which the R extension
        // launches with the VS Code server's environment. Without this, R's
        // remotes/pak see GITHUB_TOKEN and nag on every install_github:
        // "Using github PAT from envvar GITHUB_TOKEN ... use the more secure
        // credential store instead." Emptied here, they fall back to the git
        // credential store (your token, set by connect-repo) — so installs are
        // authenticated AND quiet. This does NOT affect postCreateCommand (a
        // lifecycle hook, not a terminal), so the baked-package refresh keeps
        // the built-in token and avoids GitHub API rate limits.
        // PKG_SYSREQS=false stops pak from trying to `sudo apt-get install`
        // system requirements when a student installs an R package — the
        // non-root rstudio user can't sudo, so e.g. a package pulling in V8
        // would otherwise die on "System command 'sudo' failed". The system
        // libs our packages need are provided by the base image (geospatial,
        // text-shaping, …); pak builds against those. With this off pak still
        // PRINTS any sysreq it believes is missing but does not install it.
        // NOTE: this is primarily set GLOBALLY in the student image itself
        // (ENV PKG_SYSREQS=false in the devcontainers Dockerfile), which already covers
        // the build, postCreate, and runtime terminals. It is repeated here as
        // a terminal-scoped belt-and-suspenders. (Separately, the V8 "missing
        // libnode-dev" false positive is handled in the image via a metadata
        // libnode-dev-virtual package — see the devcontainers Dockerfile.)
        "terminal.integrated.env.linux": {
          "GITHUB_TOKEN": "",
          "GH_TOKEN": "",
          "PKG_SYSREQS": "false"
        },

        // Use arf (Rust-based R console) instead of the default R terminal.
        // Point at the stable /usr/local/bin/arf symlink the base image
        // provides for exactly this purpose (see the Dockerfile in
        // PPBDS/devcontainers), NOT the real /home/rstudio/.cargo/bin/arf
        // path — that way a change to the image's default user only touches
        // that Dockerfile, not this consumer setting.
        "r.rterm.linux": "/usr/local/bin/arf",

        // Required for arf-style consoles to handle pasted code correctly.
        "r.bracketedPaste": true,

        // Render plots inside VS Code via the httpgd graphics device
        // instead of opening an external window (which doesn't work in Codespaces).
        "r.plot.useHttpgd": true,

        // Make the "Run Source" button (the ▷ in the editor title bar) and its
        // Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+S shortcut run source(FILE, echo = TRUE) instead of a
        // silent source, so students see each line echo as it executes — not
        // just the final output.
        "r.source.echo": true,

        // Don't auto-relaunch terminals when extensions contribute environment
        // variables after startup. A relaunch clears scrollback, and if it hit
        // the postAttach terminal it would wipe the welcome banner — the whole
        // point of welcome.sh. NOTE: this setting does NOT suppress the "⚠
        // extensions want to relaunch the terminal" indicator — it CAUSES it
        // (with auto-relaunch off, VS Code surfaces the pending changes as a
        // warning instead). The indicator is hidden separately below.
        "terminal.integrated.environmentChangesRelaunch": false,

        // Hide that ⚠ relaunch indicator. Pure noise for students: git auth in
        // Codespaces terminals comes from the container's global credential
        // helper (not the extension-injected env vars this warns about), we
        // deliberately blank GITHUB_TOKEN/GH_TOKEN in terminals anyway (see
        // terminal.integrated.env.linux above), and terminals opened after
        // startup get the contributed environment normally.
        "terminal.integrated.environmentChangesIndicator": "off",

        // NOTE — we want to suppress VS Code's Welcome / "Get Started" tab,
        // but there is currently NO way to do it from here. The setting that
        // controls it, workbench.startupEditor, is application-scoped: VS
        // Code only honors it in per-user settings and deliberately IGNORES
        // it in workspace/remote/devcontainer settings. So a
        // "workbench.startupEditor": "none" line here is a confirmed no-op in
        // Codespaces (see microsoft/vscode#134073, #160635, #242243) — it was
        // removed rather than left as dead config that looks like it works.
        // Revisit if that bug is ever fixed. We don't fight it further: the VS
        // Code Welcome tab is left as-is, and postAttachCommand prints a ready
        // banner (welcome.sh) pointing students to .devcontainer/STUDENT_WORKFLOW.md.

        // Auto-stage all changes on commit so students don't get a confusing
        // dialog asking whether to stage files.
        "git.enableSmartCommit": true,

        // Don't prompt "are you sure?" before push/pull.
        "git.confirmSync": false,

        // Don't inject VS Code's git-askpass into terminals (GIT_ASKPASS and
        // friends). Terminal git auth here comes from the container's
        // credential store, which connect-repo sets up (`credential.helper
        // store` + the student's token) — VS Code's injection is redundant AND
        // is one of the two extensions that make the first terminal show the
        // "⚠ extensions want to relaunch the terminal" warning (the other is
        // the closed-source GitHub Codespaces extension, no known knob). The
        // Source Control panel's own auth is a separate setting
        // (github.gitAuthentication) and is untouched. Verify after any change:
        // `connect-repo` end to end, then a terminal `git push`.
        "git.terminalAuthentication": false,

        // Fetch from the remote in the background. This was `false` (2026-07,
        // "keeps things predictable, avoids confusing status-bar changes") until
        // 2026-09-16, when reading the Git extension's source showed that ONLY
        // `true` suppresses the "Would you like Code to periodically run git
        // fetch?" popup: the prompt fires whenever autofetch is not `true` AND a
        // per-Codespace "already asked" flag is unset — so `false` and unset
        // are treated identically, and every fresh Codespace asked again on the
        // student's first push/sync. The cost of `true` is an occasional ↓N
        // status-bar arrow when the remote has commits the local doesn't, which
        // for a solo student repo essentially only happens across two
        // Codespaces. Do NOT set this back to false expecting the popup to stop.
        "git.autofetch": true,

        // Auto-save files after a short delay. Prevents the #1 beginner
        // problem: "my code isn't working" because the file wasn't saved.
        "files.autoSave": "afterDelay",

        // Stop VS Code from suggesting random extensions students don't need.
        "extensions.ignoreRecommendations": true,

        // Prevent the "open repository in parent folder?" prompt that
        // confuses students who don't understand nested git repos.
        "git.openRepositoryInParentFolders": "never",

        // Prevent extensions from opening walkthrough tabs on first install.
        "workbench.welcomePage.walkthroughs.openOnInstall": false,

        // Wrap long lines so students don't think code disappeared
        // off the right edge of the screen.
        "editor.wordWrap": "on",

        // Disable the minimap — beginners don't use it and it wastes
        // screen space, especially on Chromebooks and small laptops.
        "editor.minimap.enabled": false,

        // Prevent VS Code from opening files in "preview" mode (italic
        // tab title) which silently replaces the tab when another file
        // is clicked. Beginners think their file vanished.
        "workbench.editor.enablePreview": false
      }
    }
  }
}
